# /etc/udev/rules.d/95-cyberpunk-dock.rules
#
# Trigger /usr/local/bin/cyberpunk-dock-handler.sh on USB-C / Thunderbolt
# dock connect/disconnect.  Loaded after the system rules (numeric prefix
# 95 puts us late enough that ID_VENDOR_ID / ID_MODEL_ID / ID_SERIAL_SHORT
# have been populated by usb-id/usb-db builtins).
#
# Scope rationale — read this before broadening the match!
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# We DO NOT match `SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTRS{bDeviceClass}=="09"` (USB hub)
# broadly.  A literal USB-hub match fires for:
#   • every internal root hub at boot (xhci_hcd registers four-plus)
#   • every USB keyboard / mouse / webcam that has an internal hub
#   • every Plasma-aware monitor with a downstream USB-C hub
# Running our 3-second-sleep handler on each of those would saturate udev
# workers (`udevd: worker [N] failed`) and re-apply layouts at random.
#
# Instead, we match SPECIFIC USB vendor:product IDs known to belong to
# docks.  Add new dock entries below.  To find your dock's IDs:
#
#   lsusb                                            # list everything
#   udevadm monitor --environment --subsystem-match=usb   # plug it in
#
# Once you've added a new ID, reload rules:
#
#   sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Known docks (extend the list as needed):
#
#   17ef:3082  Lenovo ThinkPad Universal Thunderbolt 4 Dock (Gen 1)
#   17ef:3083  Lenovo ThinkPad Universal Thunderbolt 4 Smart Dock
#   17ef:3070  Lenovo ThinkPad USB-C Dock Gen 2
#   17ef:a052  Lenovo ThinkPad Hybrid USB-C with USB-A Dock
#   2109:0817  VIA Labs hub — used by some generic CalDigit / Anker docks
#
# `RUN+="…"` invokes our handler with ACTION and a derived dock-hash.
# The handler itself does the heavy lifting (sleep, kscreen-doctor,
# laptop-only fallback); the rule's only job is to FIRE narrowly.
#
# IMPORTANT: udev's RUN+= executes its target SYNCHRONOUSLY inside the
# udev worker process, with a strict timeout (180 s) and BLOCKS other
# udev events queued behind us.  Our handler `sleep 3`s then talks to
# kscreen-doctor over D-Bus — both are exactly the things udev RUN+=
# is NOT supposed to do.  Wrap with `systemd-run --no-block` so udev
# returns immediately and the handler runs untethered as a transient
# unit (logs: `journalctl -u cyberpunk-dock-*`).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Lenovo ThinkPad Universal Thunderbolt 4 Dock (Gen 1) — the T14 Gen 1
# pairs with this most often.  Both add+remove run the handler; the
# handler branches on $env{ACTION} (passed as $1) internally.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ACTION=="add|remove", ATTR{idVendor}=="17ef", ATTR{idProduct}=="3082", \
  RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block --collect /usr/local/bin/cyberpunk-dock-handler.sh %E{ACTION} 17ef-3082-%E{ID_SERIAL_SHORT}"

# Other Lenovo ThinkPad docks (commented out — uncomment whichever
# applies to your hardware).  Keeping them as commented templates makes
# the "this dock isn't recognised" diagnosis cheap: lsusb, grep here,
# uncomment, reload.
#SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ACTION=="add|remove", ATTR{idVendor}=="17ef", ATTR{idProduct}=="3083", \
#  RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block --collect /usr/local/bin/cyberpunk-dock-handler.sh %E{ACTION} 17ef-3083-%E{ID_SERIAL_SHORT}"
#SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ACTION=="add|remove", ATTR{idVendor}=="17ef", ATTR{idProduct}=="3070", \
#  RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block --collect /usr/local/bin/cyberpunk-dock-handler.sh %E{ACTION} 17ef-3070-%E{ID_SERIAL_SHORT}"
#SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ACTION=="add|remove", ATTR{idVendor}=="17ef", ATTR{idProduct}=="a052", \
#  RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block --collect /usr/local/bin/cyberpunk-dock-handler.sh %E{ACTION} 17ef-a052-%E{ID_SERIAL_SHORT}"

# Broad-pattern escape hatch — DISABLED by default.  Some non-Lenovo
# docks (CalDigit TS4, Anker 777, OWC TB4) expose only a generic VIA /
# Genesys hub chip with no dock-identifying VID:PID at the USB layer.
# In that case, the only reliable distinguisher is "USB hub class 09
# attached AT BOOT VS AT HOTPLUG".  Uncomment the rule below ONLY if
# none of the explicit matches above apply on your machine — and accept
# the trade-off that internal-keyboard hubs may also fire this handler.
#
#SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ACTION=="add|remove", ATTR{bDeviceClass}=="09", \
#  ATTR{idVendor}!="1d6b", ATTR{idVendor}!="0000", \
#  RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block --collect /usr/local/bin/cyberpunk-dock-handler.sh %E{ACTION} %E{ID_VENDOR_ID}-%E{ID_MODEL_ID}-%E{ID_SERIAL_SHORT}"
#
# 1d6b excludes Linux Foundation root hubs (xhci-internal), 0000 excludes
# unprobed hubs during early enumerate.
